    <h1>Assignment 4: Libraries!</h1>
    <p>Rebecca Liu</p>
    <h2>Capacitive Touch-Controlled Servo</h2>
    <p>
      I used a coin as a capacitive touch input. When I touch the coin,
      the servo moves to 90°. When I release it, the servo returns to 0°.
      The sketch uses the <code>CapacitiveSensor</code> library to detect
      touch and the <code>Servo</code> library to control the servo.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Schematic</h2>
      <figure>
        <img src="a4_touch_servo_schematic.png"
             alt="Schematic showing a 1 megohm resistor between Pins 2 and 4, a coin connected to Pin 4, and a servo connected to Pin 9, 5 V, and ground.">
        <figcaption>
          A 1 MΩ resistor connects the send pin (Pin 2) to the receive pin
          (Pin 4). The high resistance slows the voltage change at the
          receive pin so the library can detect a difference in timing
          when I touch the coin. The coin connects to the same junction
          as Pin 4 and one end of the resistor. The servo signal connects
          to Pin 9, with power from 5 V and ground from GND.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

