    <section>
      <h2>Additional Questions</h2>

      <h3>1. What is the voltage at Pin 9 over time?</h3>
      <figure>
        <img src="a4_q1_servo_waveform.png"
             alt="Theoretical Pin 9 waveform at 0, 90, and 180 degrees, with wider high pulses at larger commanded angles.">
        <figcaption>
          This theoretical graph shows three 100 ms excerpts from the
          sweep in Question 1. Pin 9 sends a pulse about every 20 ms,
          while the code updates the target angle by 1° every 100 ms.
          With the
          <a href="https://github.com/arduino-libraries/Servo/blob/master/src/Servo.h">
            default Arduino Servo settings
          </a>,
          the pulse stays near 5 V for about 0.54 ms at 0°, 1.47 ms at
          90°, and 2.40 ms at 180°. The signal stays near 0 V between
          pulses.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>2. How can I handle a sensor reading that is wrong 1% of the time?</h3>
      <p>
        I would take three readings close together and use the median.
        For example, if the readings are 50, 900, and 52, sorting them
        gives 50, 52, and 900. The middle value is 52, so the isolated
        spike has little effect on the result.
      </p>
      <pre><code>readings = []

repeat 3 times:
    readings.append(readSensor())

sort readings from lowest to highest
return readings[1]  // Index 1 is the middle value.</code></pre>

      <h3>3. How can I reduce random measurement noise of ±10%?</h3>
      <p>
        I would average ten readings collected over a short time. If the
        true input stays roughly stable, readings above and below it can
        partly cancel each other out. For example, readings of 90, 103,
        and 107 average to 100.
      </p>
      <pre><code>total = 0

repeat 10 times:
    total = total + readSensor()

return total / 10.0  // Divide by the number of readings.</code></pre>

<h3>4. Did I use AI tools?</h3>

<p>
  Yes. I used ChatGPT to clarify a few concepts while working on this
  assignment. I drew the final waveform and circuit schematic myself,
  and I wrote my own answer to Question 2.
</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Servo waveform:</strong> I asked where the 0.54 ms pulse
    width came from and how to show it on the graph. ChatGPT explained
    how the Servo library maps angles to pulse widths and showed me
    how those widths fit on the time axis. I used that explanation to
    draw my own graph.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Question 2:</strong> I asked how taking the median helps
    when a sensor occasionally gives a wrong reading. ChatGPT walked
    me through an example with three readings. After I understood why
    the middle value is less affected by an extreme reading, I wrote
    my own answer.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Circuit schematic:</strong> I asked how to draw the wire
    from the coin. ChatGPT explained that the coin uses a single wire
    to the Pin 4 junction, where it connects to one end of the
    resistor. I drew that connection in my schematic.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  The waveform explanation helped me separate the time the signal
  stays high from the time the servo takes to move. That made it
  easier to decide how wide to draw each pulse.
</p>
